<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?> 

<section xml:id="section_1_2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Preliminary: Exploring Data</title>

<p>In this section, step 3 of the Statistical Investigation Process is further explored. 
  The goal is to describe, measure, and, if possible, explain the patterns of variation 
  (the <em>distribution</em>) in the values of the variable.
</p>

<subsection> 
  <title>Graphs for quantitative variables</title>
  <p>
    A <term>dotplot</term> is a graph with one dot representing the variable outcome 
    for each observational unit. Dotplots, like the graph of the performance lengths 
    in the animation above, are good for smaller datasets. Another common graph for a 
    quantitative variable is a histogram. A <term>histogram</term> separates the 
    quantitative variable into bins and displays the number of observations in each 
    bin.
  </p>
</subsection>

<subsection> 
  <title>Describing the distribution</title>
  <p>
    Four important features are discussed when describing the distribution of a 
    quantitative variable:
  </p>
  <p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The <term>shape</term> of a distribution is described by the number of 
        peaks and whether one side of the distribution tends to stretch out longer 
        than the other.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <term>center</term> of a distribution is the middle or typical value.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <term>variability</term> of a distribution is the spread in the observations.
      </li>
      <li>
        <term>Unusual observations</term> are observations that do not follow the overall 
        pattern of the distribution.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</subsection>

</section>